page("reverse-sear-steak-small-grill","Ribeye on a Small Charcoal Grill: Sear and Slide","A thick ribeye on the Slot Grill Compact using a two-zone fire: dry brine, hard sear, gentle finish, resting and slicing. With doneness chart, variations and troubleshooting.",IMG["c08"],recipe2(
"<p>A thick, well-marbled ribeye is the best argument for cooking over charcoal, and a small grill is a great tool for it: the food sits close to the coals, and the 12-gauge carbon steel of the Slot Grill Compact holds heat for a hard sear. The catch is thickness. A 1¼\" steak left over hot coals the whole time chars outside before the middle comes up. The answer is <b>sear and slide</b>: crust it over the hot zone, then slide it to the cool zone to finish gently. You get a deep crust and an even, rosy middle without a lid, a dial or guesswork.</p>",
"2","About 30 minutes active, plus optional 40+ minutes salting",
["2 ribeye steaks, about 1¼\" thick (bone-in or boneless)","Coarse salt, about ¾ teaspoon per steak","Freshly ground black pepper","High-smoke-point oil","Optional: 2 tablespoons butter, 2 cloves garlic, a sprig of rosemary or thyme","Optional for serving: flaky salt"],
["<b>Dry brine.</b> At least 40 minutes before cooking (or up to overnight, uncovered on a rack in the fridge), salt both sides and edges. If you're short on time, salt right before cooking instead. Salting 10–20 minutes ahead is the least effective window.","<b>Light a full chimney.</b> Steak wants a hot zone. Lump charcoal is great here.","<b>Build two zones.</b> When the coals are ashed over, pile them across two-thirds of the Compact's firebox, deeper at the far end. Leave the last third empty.","<b>Preheat and oil the grates</b> over the coals for several minutes.","<b>Pat the steaks dry,</b> rub with a thin film of oil, and add pepper.","<b>Sear.</b> Lay the steaks over the deepest coals. Leave them until they release, about 2 minutes, then flip. Sear the second side. Hold the fat cap edge against the grate with tongs for a minute to render it.","<b>Slide.</b> Move the steaks to the cool end. Flip every minute or two and check the thickest part with an instant-read thermometer.","<b>Pull early.</b> Take the steaks off a few degrees below your target; the temperature keeps rising as they rest.","<b>Optional butter baste.</b> Set a small pan or the griddle at the cool end, melt butter with garlic and herbs, and spoon it over the steaks for the last minute.","<b>Rest</b> 5 minutes on a clean board, then slice across the grain and sprinkle with flaky salt."],
why="<ul><li><b>Close, intense heat</b> for a fast crust.</li><li><b>Thick steel</b> that holds heat when cold meat hits the grate.</li><li><b>A two-piece top</b>, so you can run a grate plus a <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> for a butter baste or onions.</li><li><b>A cool zone</b> that works like the oven half of a restaurant sear-and-finish.</li></ul>",
extra="<h2>Doneness guide (pull temperatures)</h2><p>Many cooks pull steak at these temperatures and let it rise during the rest. USDA's minimum for beef steaks is 145°F with a 3-minute rest; choose what you're comfortable with.</p><div class='tbl'><table><tr><th>Doneness</th><th>Pull at about</th><th>After rest about</th></tr><tr><td>Rare</td><td>120°F</td><td>125°F</td></tr><tr><td>Medium-rare</td><td>125–130°F</td><td>130–135°F</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>135–140°F</td><td>140–145°F</td></tr><tr><td>Medium-well and up</td><td>145°F+</td><td>150°F+</td></tr></table></div>",
variations=["<b>Strip or sirloin:</b> same method; leaner steaks flare less and cook a little faster.","<b>Thin steaks (under 1\"):</b> skip the slide. Sear hot on both sides and pull.","<b>Reverse order (true reverse sear):</b> start on the cool side until about 15°F below target, then sear hard over the coals. Great for very thick steaks.","<b>Steak and eggs:</b> run a griddle on the cool side and fry eggs while the steaks rest. See <a href='/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html'>camp breakfast</a>.","<b>Wood flavor:</b> add a small chunk of untreated hardwood to the hot coals right before searing."],
trouble=[("Gray band under the crust","Too long over hot coals. Sear shorter, finish on the cool side."),("Crust won't form","Steak wet or coals not ready. Pat dry; wait for ashed coals; preheat grates."),("Big flare-ups from the fat","Move to the cool side until they stop; render the fat cap edge-on briefly."),("Overshot the temperature","Pull earlier next time; carryover during the rest adds several degrees.")],
serve="<p>Slice across the grain. Leftover steak makes the best <a href='/steak-fajitas-compact-grill.html'>fajitas</a> or steak-and-egg tacos the next morning. Refrigerate leftovers within 2 hours. Once the grill is cold, scrape the grates and wipe them with a light coat of oil.</p>"),"recipes",
[("How long do I cook a ribeye on a small charcoal grill?","It depends on thickness and fire, so cook to temperature. A 1¼ inch ribeye typically gets about 2 minutes per side over hot coals, then several more minutes on the cool side until it reaches your target internal temperature."),("Do I need a lid to cook a thick steak?","No. A two-zone fire lets you sear over direct heat and finish over indirect heat on an open grill like the Slot Grill Compact."),("When should I salt steak?","At least 40 minutes before cooking, or right before cooking if you are short on time."),("What temperature should I pull a medium-rare steak?","Many cooks pull at about 125 to 130°F and rest it to about 130 to 135°F. USDA's minimum for beef steaks is 145°F with a 3-minute rest.")])

page("smash-burgers-on-a-griddle","Smash Burgers on a Small Charcoal Griddle","Crispy-edged smash burgers on a carbon steel Slot Grill griddle over charcoal: beef blend, ball size, smashing technique, cheese melt, buns and onions.",IMG["pl2"],recipe2(
"<p>Smash burgers might be the perfect small-grill food. They cook in minutes, they need a ripping hot flat surface (which a carbon steel griddle over charcoal is), and you can turn out a stack of them in waves from a 146 sq in grill. The method is simple: a ball of cold beef hits a very hot griddle, you smash it thin immediately, and the wide contact makes a lacy, deeply browned crust. Here's how to do it on the Slot Grill Compact with a <a href='"+GRID+"'>Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a>.</p>",
"2–3 (6 patties)","About 25 minutes",
["1 lb 80/20 ground beef, kept cold, divided into six 2½–3 oz loose balls","Salt and freshly ground black pepper","6 slices American cheese (melts best)","3–6 soft buns (potato buns are classic)","1 onion, sliced paper thin (optional)","Pickles, mustard, ketchup or your sauce","Butter for toasting buns (optional)","A little high-smoke-point oil","Parchment squares (optional, to stop sticking)"],
["<b>Prep everything first.</b> Smash burgers move fast. Have buns opened, cheese unwrapped, sauce made and the spatula ready before the beef hits the steel.","<b>Light a full chimney</b> and spread the coals in a fairly even bed under the griddle side, with a little cool area under the grate side.","<b>Preheat the griddle</b> thoroughly over the coals. A drop of water should skitter and evaporate almost instantly.","<b>Oil lightly</b> and wipe to a thin film.","<b>Place 2–3 beef balls</b> on the hottest part, spaced well apart.","<b>Smash immediately and hard</b> with a sturdy metal spatula (lay parchment on top to prevent sticking), to about ¼\" thick. Hold a few seconds. Smash only once, in the first 30 seconds; after that you squeeze out juice.","<b>Season</b> the top with salt and pepper. Add thin onions on top if using, pressing them in.","<b>Cook without moving</b> until the edges are deep brown and lacy, about 1½–2 minutes.","<b>Scrape under the patty</b> with the spatula edge, keeping the crust on the meat, and flip.","<b>Add cheese</b> immediately, and stack two patties if you like doubles. Cook about 30 seconds more.","<b>Toast buns</b> on the grate side or the cool part of the griddle while the patties cook.","<b>Build and serve right away.</b> Scrape the griddle, re-oil, and run the next wave. Ground beef should reach 160°F."],
why="<ul><li>The griddle is the same thick carbon steel as the grill and holds heat when cold meat hits it.</li><li>The Compact's two-piece top lets you run a griddle on one side for patties and a grate on the other for toasting buns.</li><li>A small grill heats up fast and runs hot, which is exactly what a smash burger needs.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Oklahoma-style onion burger:</b> pile a big handful of paper-thin onions onto the ball and smash through it.","<b>Breakfast smash:</b> top with a fried egg from the same griddle.","<b>Spicy:</b> add sliced jalapeños to the griddle and press them into the patty.","<b>Patty melt:</b> serve on buttered griddled bread with grilled onions.","<b>Crowd mode:</b> cook in waves; see <a href='/cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill.html'>cooking for a crowd</a>."],
trouble=[("Patties stick and tear","Griddle not hot or not seasoned; smash with parchment; scrape firmly under the crust"),("No crust","Griddle not hot enough or beef too warm. More coals under the griddle; keep beef cold."),("Dry burgers","Smashed too late or pressed again. Smash once, early."),("Griddle cools after first batch","Add lit coals under it between waves; scrape off grease.")],
serve="<p>Burgers are best eaten straight off the griddle. Between waves, scrape grease into a heat-safe container (not onto the coals, which causes flare-ups). When the griddle is cool, scrape, wipe and oil it lightly to keep its seasoning.</p>"),"recipes",
[("Do I need a griddle for smash burgers on a charcoal grill?","Yes. Smash burgers need a solid flat surface. A Slot Grill griddle (plancha) fits the Compact and is made of the same thick carbon steel as the grill. A cast-iron skillet also works."),("What size balls for smash burgers?","About 2½ to 3 ounces each. Two thin patties stacked make a classic double."),("Why do my smash burgers stick?","Usually the griddle isn't hot enough or well seasoned. Preheat thoroughly, oil lightly, smash with parchment on top, and scrape firmly under the crust to flip."),("What temperature should a smash burger reach?","USDA recommends 160°F for ground beef. Thin smash patties get there quickly.")])
